   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.10",
    "topic_title": "Origins of Cell Compartmentalization",
    "stem": "Which statement correctly explains the evolutionary origin of mitochondria and chloroplasts and compares compartmentalization in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts arose solely from folds of the host cell's membrane; prokaryotes typically have specialized internal regions without membrane-bound organelles, whereas eukaryotes partition their cells with internal membranes.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Membrane-folding trap: this substitutes changes in the host's membrane for the incorporation of formerly free-living cells. Endosymbiotic theory identifies prokaryotic ancestors as the source of mitochondria and chloroplasts. Fix: Check whether the proposed origin includes formerly independent prokaryotic cells."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts evolved from free-living prokaryotes through endosymbiosis; prokaryotes have no specialized internal regions, whereas eukaryotes partition their cells with internal membranes.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "No-organization trap: this equates lacking membrane-bound organelles with lacking internal organization. Prokaryotes can have specialized internal regions even though they typically lack membrane-bound organelles. Fix: Separate the presence of specialized regions from the presence of surrounding membranes."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "All eukaryotic membrane-bound organelles evolved from free-living prokaryotes through endosymbiosis; prokaryotes typically have specialized internal regions without membrane-bound organelles, whereas eukaryotes partition their cells with internal membranes.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Overgeneralization trap: this extends the endosymbiotic origin of mitochondria and chloroplasts to every membrane-bound organelle. Having a surrounding membrane does not establish that an organelle descended from a free-living prokaryote. Fix: Identify which organelles the endosymbiotic explanation applies to before generalizing."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts evolved from free-living prokaryotes through endosymbiosis; prokaryotes typically have specialized internal regions without membrane-bound organelles, whereas eukaryotes partition their cells with internal membranes.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Endosymbiosis explains how formerly free-living prokaryotic cells became organelles within host cells. The comparison also distinguishes specialized regions in prokaryotes from membrane-enclosed compartments in eukaryotes without implying that prokaryotes lack internal organization."
     }
    ]
   },
